Chapter: BuiltinAlloc
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 What does one call to a list builtin retain, and does that answer change
 when the input gets bigger? That is the measurement CostModel.md 5.1 names
 as the block on the `fixed` rung: separating "fixed bytes per call" from
 "one walk over an input" cannot be done from the code's shape, because a
 body with no loop in it can still call something that copies its argument.
 3.1 published the text family and nothing else. This is the list family.

 THE DISCRIMINATOR IS INPUT SIZE, NOT ITERATION COUNT, which is what makes
 this instrument different from `accumulator-corpus` and `literal-alloc`.
 Those run a loop n times and read the growth. Here every arm makes ONE
 call, and the two readings differ only in how large the argument is. A
 builtin that retains the same bytes on a list of 64 and a list of 256 is
 `fixed`; one that retains four times as much is walking its input.

 `list-push` GETS TWO ARMS AND THAT IS THE POINT. `__list_snoc` has three
 paths (`DevelopersGuide.md`, "list-push is NOT unconditionally
 destructive"): store in spare capacity, extend in place when the list is
 the topmost allocation, or COPY to a fresh allocation. Which one runs is
 positional, decided by the call site's allocation order and enforced by
 nothing. A cost class has to be the worst case over structure, so the
 buried arm -- one small allocation made after the list, which is all it
 takes -- is the arm that decides the class. Reading only the topmost arm
 would publish `fixed` for a builtin that copies.

 The control arm makes no call at all, so the harness's own retention is
 visible and every row is read against it rather than against zero.

 We say:

Section: The Instrument

  keep : List Integer -> Integer
  keep (xs) = list-length xs

  mk-list : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mk-list (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else mk-list n (i + 1) (list-push acc i)

  ratio-tenths : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ratio-tenths (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n <= 0 then 0
    else (at-4n * 10) / at-n

  show-tenths : Integer -> Text
  show-tenths (t) = show (t / 10) & "." & show (t - (t / 10) * 10)

 The class this instrument can report, and it deliberately cannot report
 `growing`: one call cannot be nested in a walk, so the top of the lattice
 is not a question a single call answers.

  klass : Integer, Integer -> Text
  klass (at-n) (at-4n) =
    if at-n == 0 & at-4n == 0 then "none"
    else if at-n == at-4n then "fixed"
    else "input"

  row : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  row (name) (at-n) (at-4n) =
    name & " n=" & show at-n & " 4n=" & show at-4n
      & " x" & show-tenths (ratio-tenths at-n at-4n)
      & " " & klass at-n at-4n

Section: Arms

  m-control : Integer -> Integer
  m-control (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep xs
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-length : Integer -> Integer
  m-length (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length xs
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-at : Integer -> Integer
  m-at (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-at xs (n / 2)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-push-topmost : Integer -> Integer
  m-push-topmost (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-push xs 7)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 One three-element list built after the input is enough to move it off the
 top, which is the whole of the aliasing rule in the guide.

  m-push-buried : Integer -> Integer
  m-push-buried (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-push xs 7) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 THE ARM THAT SHOWS THIS INSTRUMENT CAN SAY "CHEAP". `base` is 64 and 256,
 both powers of two, and `mk-list` leaves capacity equal to length there, so
 the very next push always lands on the doubling boundary and pays the whole
 capacity. That is the worst case and it is the right number for a CEILING,
 but an instrument that can only ever report it is not measuring, it is
 asserting. One extra element puts the list at length n+1 in a backing array
 of 2n, and the same call then takes the spare-capacity path.

  m-push-spare : Integer -> Integer
  m-push-spare (n) =
    let xs = mk-list (n + 1) 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-push xs 7)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-set-at : Integer -> Integer
  m-set-at (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-set-at xs (n / 2) 7) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-insert-at : Integer -> Integer
  m-insert-at (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (list-insert-at xs (n / 2) 7) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-tail : Integer -> Integer
  m-tail (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = keep (__list-tail xs) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Text Family

 The published Text table (`DevelopersGuide.md`, 2026-08-15) measured each of
 these at ONE input size, which is enough to say "it allocates" and not enough
 to say which class it is. `substring` reading "16 bytes per call at n = 4" is
 the case in point: 16 bytes is a fact about that call, and whether it is a
 CEILING depends on which of the two lengths it tracks.

 SO SUBSTRING GETS TWO ARMS, VARYING DIFFERENT THINGS, and the pair is the
 finding rather than either number. One holds the OUTPUT at four characters and
 grows the input; the other takes half the input and grows both. If the cost
 follows the output, a caller taking a fixed-size field out of a line of any
 length is `fixed` and can say so, which is most of the parsing in this tree.
 If it follows the input, none of them can.

  mk-text : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  mk-text (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else mk-text n (i + 1) (acc & "x")

  m-text-length : Integer -> Integer
  m-text-length (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length s
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-sub-fixed-out : Integer -> Integer
  m-sub-fixed-out (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (substring s 0 4) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-sub-half-out : Integer -> Integer
  m-sub-half-out (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (substring s 0 (n / 2)) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-contains : Integer -> Integer
  m-contains (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if text-contains s "xx" then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-starts : Integer -> Integer
  m-starts (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if text-starts-with s "xx" then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-compare : Integer -> Integer
  m-compare (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let t = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-compare s t
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-itoa : Integer -> Integer
  m-itoa (n) =
    let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (integer-to-text n) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 `integer-to-text`'s input size is DIGITS, and the arm above varies its
 argument from 64 to 256, which is two digits to three. That is not a 4x
 spread in the thing that could matter, so on its own it would be a `fixed`
 claim resting on almost no range. This one runs the fourth power: 8 digits
 against 10, and read together with the arm above it covers 2, 3, 8 and 10.

  m-itoa-big : Integer -> Integer
  m-itoa-big (n) =
    let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (integer-to-text (n * n * n * n)) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-char-to-text : Integer -> Integer
  m-char-to-text (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (char-to-text (char-at s 0)) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Integer And Bit Family

 These take Integers, so there is no input to walk and the reading is expected
 to be zero at both sizes. That expectation is exactly why they are measured
 rather than declared: `bs-alloc` may only carry a value somebody read, and a
 shape-only rule would have accepted `negate` on a real for two months.

 The magnitude still varies 64 against 256 for the same reason every other arm
 does, so a builtin that boxed its result would show it.

 A ZERO HERE COULD MEAN THE CALL NEVER HAPPENED, and the run carries its own
 answer to that. `integer-to-text` takes the same constant-shaped argument
 these arms take and reads 16 bytes, so a builtin call with a literal operand
 is not folded away before the heap marks see it.

  m-bit-and : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-and (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-and n 12
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bit-or : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-or (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-or n 12
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bit-xor : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-xor (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-xor n 12
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bit-shl : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-shl (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-shl n 3
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bit-shr : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-shr (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-shr n 3
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bit-shru : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-shru (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-shru n 3
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bit-not : Integer -> Integer
  m-bit-not (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = bit-not n
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-int-mod : Integer -> Integer
  m-int-mod (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = int-mod n 7
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-int-rem : Integer -> Integer
  m-int-rem (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = int-rem n 7
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-abs : Integer -> Integer
  m-abs (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = abs (0 - n)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-min : Integer -> Integer
  m-min (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = min n 7
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-max : Integer -> Integer
  m-max (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = max n 7
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-negate-int : Integer -> Integer
  m-negate-int (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = negate n
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 THE REAL ARMS ARE READ AS A PAIR, not on their own. A Real cannot close the
 `+ r - r` bracket, so every real reading carries a conversion in and out with
 it; the conversion arm is the control and `negate`'s own class is the
 difference between the two rows. Reading the second row alone would charge
 `negate` for whatever the conversions cost.

  m-real-round-trip : Integer -> Integer
  m-real-round-trip (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (real-from-int n)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-negate-real : Integer -> Integer
  m-negate-real (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (negate (real-from-int n))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: Predicates And Conversions

 The character predicates and the small conversions. Each is expected to be
 `none`, and each is measured for the reason the integer family was: the
 registry may only carry a value somebody read, and the whole point of the
 rung is that a shape-only rule cannot tell these from `substring`.

 `show` and `text-split` are in this section deliberately even though they
 plainly allocate. A section of nothing but zeros is a section whose
 instrument nobody has checked, and these two are the ones that make the
 zeros beside them mean something.

  m-is-letter : Integer -> Integer
  m-is-letter (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if is-letter (char-at s 0) then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-is-digit : Integer -> Integer
  m-is-digit (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if is-digit (char-at s 0) then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-is-whitespace : Integer -> Integer
  m-is-whitespace (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if is-whitespace (char-at s 0) then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-code-to-char : Integer -> Integer
  m-code-to-char (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = char-code (code-to-char 65)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-text-to-integer : Integer -> Integer
  m-text-to-integer (n) =
    let s = show n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-to-integer s
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-compare-int : Integer -> Integer
  m-compare-int (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = compare n 7
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-show : Integer -> Integer
  m-show (n) =
    let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = text-length (show n) + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-text-split : Integer -> Integer
  m-text-split (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let after = mk-text 3 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length (text-split s "x") + text-length after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: Raw Memory

 The peek and poke family reads and writes through a pointer and cannot
 allocate, but "cannot" is the word this whole chapter exists to replace with
 a reading. The buffer is allocated BEFORE the mark so its own cost is not
 charged to the accessor, which is the same discipline every arm above uses
 for its input.

  m-peek-byte : Integer -> Integer
  m-peek-byte (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = peek-byte buf 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-peek-32 : Integer -> Integer
  m-peek-32 (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = peek-32 buf 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-poke-byte : Integer -> Integer
  m-poke-byte (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = poke-byte buf 0 65
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-poke-32 : Integer -> Integer
  m-poke-32 (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = poke-32 buf 0 65
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

 IT TAKES `n` AND NOT A LITERAL, and the first version of this arm took the
 literal 64. It duly read `fixed` at both sizes -- which is a true statement
 about the arm and says nothing whatever about the builtin, because the only
 thing that varies here is the argument the caller chose. `alloc-bytes` is
 `substring`'s shape: its class follows a parameter, so an arm that holds that
 parameter constant cannot see the class at all.

  m-alloc-bytes : Integer -> Integer
  m-alloc-bytes (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = alloc-bytes n
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Buffer Family

 `__buf-read-bytes` IS THE ONE ROW HERE THAT SOMEBODY ALREADY BELIEVES A
 NUMBER ABOUT. `CLAUDE.md` rule 8 lists it under red flags as an "8x blowup",
 and four designs cite that figure as settled -- `ProtocolStack` twice,
 `ComplianceEvidence`, `EffectRows`. Nothing had measured it. A number carried
 in the file every session loads, cited across a campaign and never read off
 the machine, is exactly the shape this project keeps paying for.

 The arithmetic behind the belief is sound and that is what makes it worth
 checking rather than assuming: a byte read into a `List Integer` occupies a
 full element, so N bytes become N elements of eight bytes plus whatever the
 list header and capacity policy add. Whether the answer is 8x, or 8x plus a
 constant, or 16x because capacity doubles, is a question only the machine
 settles -- and the difference decides whether a caller can afford it.

 The buffer is allocated before the mark in every arm, so its own cost is not
 charged to the accessor being measured.

  m-buf-write-byte : Integer -> Integer
  m-buf-write-byte (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (n * 8)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = __buf-write-byte buf 0 65
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-buf-write-bytes : Integer -> Integer
  m-buf-write-bytes (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (n * 8)
    in let src = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = __buf-write-bytes buf 0 src
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-buf-read-bytes : Integer -> Integer
  m-buf-read-bytes (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (n * 8)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length (__buf-read-bytes buf 0 n)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-list-with-capacity : Integer -> Integer
  m-list-with-capacity (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length (__list-with-capacity n)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-narrow : Integer -> Integer
  m-narrow (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (n * 8)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = __narrow (peek-byte buf 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Pointer And Container Family

 THE PROOF TERMS ARE NOT ARMED HERE AND THE REASON IS THE POINT. `Refl`,
 `assume`, `sym`, `trans`, `cong` and `app-cong` all lower through
 `emit-proof-builtin`, which is `emit-int-lit st 0`: they are ERASED to a zero
 literal and there is no allocation path in any of them to find. That is a
 structural determination from the emitter rather than a reading, on the same
 footing as `list-set-at`'s entry in the guide, and it is the honest way to
 classify them -- this instrument cannot arm them at all, because a proof term
 cannot close the `+ r - r` bracket that forces its own result to be used. An
 arm that bound one and threw it away would be measuring dead code elimination.

 What IS armed here is the family that returns integers or containers.

 THE TWO LINKED-LIST ROWS CARRY A BRIDGE AND THEIR ABSOLUTE NUMBERS INCLUDE
 IT. A `LinkedList` cannot close the `+ r - r` bracket either, so both arms go
 through `__linked-list-to-list` to reach a length, and whatever that
 conversion retains is inside both readings. The CLASS is unaffected and the
 class is what the registry stores: both rows read the same bytes at 64 and at
 256, so neither scales with the list. `__linked-list-to-list` itself stays
 `unknown` rather than being inferred from the difference between two arms
 that both contain it.

  m-tag-equal : Integer -> Integer
  m-tag-equal (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if tag-equal xs xs then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-variant-tag : Integer -> Integer
  m-variant-tag (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = variant-tag xs
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-address-of : Integer -> Integer
  m-address-of (n) =
    let xs = mk-list n 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = address-of xs
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-memset : Integer -> Integer
  m-memset (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes (n * 8)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = peek-byte buf 0 + (let z = __memset buf 0 8 in 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-linked-empty : Integer -> Integer
  m-linked-empty (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length (__linked-list-to-list (__linked-list-empty n))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-linked-push : Integer -> Integer
  m-linked-push (n) =
    let ll = __linked-list-empty n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = list-length (__linked-list-to-list (__linked-list-push ll 7))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Real Conversion Family

 Sixteen rows, eight arms, and the reason it is not sixteen arms is the one
 thing here worth writing down. A Real of any width cannot close the
 `+ r - r` bracket, so every reading is a CHAIN: an integer goes in, some
 conversions run, an integer comes out. A chain therefore charges its arm for
 everything in it, which is what forced `negate-real` to be read against
 `real-roundtrip` rather than against zero.

 THE LADDER TURNS THAT INTO AN ADVANTAGE. Allocation cannot be negative, so a
 chain that reads EQUAL to a control containing a subset of it proves every
 added member is zero, all at once. `real-from-int` and `real-to-int` are
 already `none` (measured 2026-08-21), so `real-roundtrip` above is the base
 of the ladder, and each arm below adds one pair to a rung already read.

 The f32 rungs sit on top of the f64 ones on purpose: the approx-trapping and
 approx-saturating pairs need an f32 to work on, and the only way to reach one
 from an Integer is through a pair the rung below has already settled. An arm
 that reads nonzero anywhere on the ladder pins the cost to what IT added, and
 nothing above it may be read until that is resolved.

  m-approx-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-approx-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (from-real-approx (to-real-approx (real-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-trapping-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-trapping-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (from-real-trapping (to-real-trapping (real-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-saturating-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-saturating-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (from-real-saturating (to-real-saturating (real-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-approx-int-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-approx-int-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-approx-to-int (real-approx-from-int n)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-approx-trapping-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-approx-trapping-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-approx-to-int (from-real-approx-trapping (to-real-approx-trapping (real-approx-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-approx-saturating-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-approx-saturating-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-approx-to-int (from-real-approx-saturating (to-real-approx-saturating (real-approx-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-bits-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-bits-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (bits-to-real (real-to-bits (real-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-approx-bits-pair : Integer -> Integer
  m-approx-bits-pair (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-approx-to-int (bits-to-real-approx (real-approx-to-bits (real-approx-from-int n)))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The SIMD Family

 A Vector closes the `+ r - r` bracket no better than a Real does, so this
 section is the same ladder one step further out: `vec-extract` and
 `vec-reduce-add` are the only ways back to a scalar, and `real-to-int` carries
 the scalar the rest of the way. Both ends were measured at zero before this
 section was written, which is what lets a rung charge only what it added.

 THE EXTRACTORS ARE MEASURED FIRST AND ALONE, and every rung below depends on
 their answer. `vec-extract` is inside the mark of every arm here, because it
 is the only way out of a vector; if it allocated, every reading in this
 section would carry it and nothing would be attributable. So its own arm
 builds the vector BEFORE the mark, and the same for `vec4-extract`.

 THE MASK IS BUILT BEFORE THE MARK TOO, and that is the whole of what makes
 the four predicate rows readable. A `VectorMask` comes from comparing two
 vectors, which is an operator and not a name in the registry; a mask built
 inside the mark charges `mask-any` and its three siblings for the
 comparison's box. The first version of this arm did exactly that and read 16
 bytes for four builtins that return a Boolean or an Integer.

 `vec-load-at` and `vec-store-at` take an address, so their buffer is
 allocated before the mark like every other accessor here.

 WHAT THE SECTION FOUND: EVERY VECTOR THIS FAMILY PRODUCES IS BOXED, at 16
 bytes, and the split falls exactly along produce-versus-read. Eight names
 return a vector and every one of them reads 16 at both sizes: `vec-splat`,
 the four arithmetic names, `vec-select`, `vec-load-at`, `vec4-splat`. Nine
 read out of one and allocate nothing. So a caller that keeps a vector in a
 register across a chain of operations is paying a box per step, and `bounded
 none` cannot construct a vector at all -- there is no cheap way to make one,
 only to read one it was handed.

  m-vec-extract : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-extract (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract x 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec4-extract : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec4-extract (n) =
    let q = vec4-splat (real-approx-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (from-real-approx (vec4-extract q 0))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-splat : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-splat (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-splat (real-from-int n)) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-add : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-add (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-add x x) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-sub : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-sub (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-sub x x) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-mul : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-mul (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-mul x x) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-div : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-div (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-div x x) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-reduce : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-reduce (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-reduce-add x)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-mask : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-mask (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let y = vec-splat (real-from-int (n + 1))
    in let m = x < y
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = mask-count m + (if mask-any m then 1 else 0)
                 + (if mask-all m then 2 else 0) + (if mask-none m then 4 else 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-select : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-select (n) =
    let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let y = vec-splat (real-from-int (n + 1))
    in let m = x < y
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-select m x y) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-load : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-load (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 16
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (vec-extract (vec-load-at buf) 0)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-store : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-store (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 16
    in let x = vec-splat (real-from-int n)
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-store-at buf x
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec4 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec4 (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = real-to-int (from-real-approx (vec4-extract (vec4-splat (real-approx-from-int n)) 0))
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-width : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-width (n) =
    let x = real-from-int n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = suggested-vector-width x
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Sized Vector Family

 A DIFFERENT `Vector` FROM THE SECTION ABOVE. Those are SIMD lanes of a fixed
 machine width; these five are the sized-vector builtins, whose value has a
 list's layout, the count at offset 0 and elements from offset 8, and whose
 LENGTH IS PART OF THE TYPE.

 THAT IS WHY THESE ARMS DO NOT SCALE THEIR INPUT WITH `n`, AND THE HARNESS'S
 USUAL DISCRIMINATOR CANNOT BE USED HERE. `mk-list` cannot have a sized-vector
 twin: a recursive builder returns `Vector 0` on one branch and `Vector 1` on
 the other and the checker refuses it (CDX2001), so there is no way to spell a
 vector whose length is a runtime value. Each arm takes its size from the SHAPE
 it is written in, and the two readings a scaling row needs come from two
 separately spelled vectors, `v4` and `v16`.

 So `vec-empty` and `vec-singleton` reading the same bytes at both sizes is
 NOT an arm that failed to vary: neither takes a vector at all, and
 `vec-singleton` takes exactly one element by its type. There is no larger
 input to give them. `vec-head` and `vec-length` DO take one, and are read at
 4 and at 16 for that reason, which is what makes their zeroes worth having.

 WHAT THE SECTION FOUND: produce-versus-read again, and `vec-cons` is the only
 row that walks. `vec-empty` retains 16 flat and `vec-singleton` 24. `vec-head`
 and `vec-length` retain nothing at either size, which is what lets the two
 producer rows be charged only for what they add. `vec-cons` reads 80 bytes
 onto a 4-vector and 272 onto a 16-vector, so it copies. It reaches
 `__list_snoc` through the same buried arm `list-push` uses, because which of
 that helper's three paths runs is positional and a cost class has to be the
 worst case over structure.

  v4 : Integer -> Vector 4 Integer
  v4 (n) = vec-cons 4 (vec-cons 3 (vec-cons 2 (vec-singleton n)))

  v16 : Integer -> Vector 16 Integer
  v16 (n) = vec-cons 16 (vec-cons 15 (vec-cons 14 (vec-cons 13 (vec-cons 12 (vec-cons 11 (vec-cons 10 (vec-cons 9 (vec-cons 8 (vec-cons 7 (vec-cons 6 (vec-cons 5 (vec-cons 4 (vec-cons 3 (vec-cons 2 (vec-singleton n)))))))))))))))

  m-vec-empty : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-empty (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-length vec-empty
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-singleton : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-singleton (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-length (vec-singleton n)
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-length-4 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-length-4 (n) =
    let v = v4 n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-length v
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-length-16 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-length-16 (n) =
    let v = v16 n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-length v
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-head-4 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-head-4 (n) =
    let v = v4 n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-head v
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-head-16 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-head-16 (n) =
    let v = v16 n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-head v
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-cons-4 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-cons-4 (n) =
    let v = v4 n
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-length (vec-cons 7 v) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-vec-cons-16 : Integer -> Integer
  m-vec-cons-16 (n) =
    let v = v16 n
    in let after = mk-list 3 0 []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = vec-length (vec-cons 7 v) + keep after
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Atomics And The Fence

 Six names that take an address and return an Integer or a Boolean, so this
 section needs no ladder: every arm closes its own bracket. The word they are
 aimed at is `bs-varies`, which reads True on all six because their ANSWER
 depends on what another core did. That is orthogonal to what they retain, and
 the registry stores the two separately; a builtin whose result varies is not
 thereby a builtin whose allocation varies.

 The buffer is allocated before the mark like every other accessor here, and
 one arm runs before the others so the cell holds a value we put there rather
 than whatever `alloc-bytes` handed back.

 THE FIVE SIZED-VECTOR NAMES ARE NOT ARMED AND THE REASON IS NOT COST.
 `vec-empty`, `vec-singleton`, `vec-head`, `vec-cons` and `vec-length` are
 broken: one is CDX2040 unresolved, one answers wrong, one faults, and the
 remaining two have no working producer to reach them through. Nothing in the
 tree calls any of them, which is why nobody knew. The account and the three
 mechanisms are in `docs/PM/CurrentPlan.md` under the unowned registers. They
 stay `unknown` here, because a row may only carry a value somebody measured
 and a builtin that faults or lies cannot be measured.

  m-atomic-store : Integer -> Integer
  m-atomic-store (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 8
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = atomic-store buf n
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-atomic-load : Integer -> Integer
  m-atomic-load (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 8
    in let z = atomic-store buf n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = atomic-load buf
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r + z - z

  m-atomic-add : Integer -> Integer
  m-atomic-add (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 8
    in let z = atomic-store buf n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = atomic-add buf 3
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r + z - z

  m-atomic-exchange : Integer -> Integer
  m-atomic-exchange (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 8
    in let z = atomic-store buf n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = atomic-exchange buf 9
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r + z - z

  m-atomic-cas : Integer -> Integer
  m-atomic-cas (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 8
    in let z = atomic-store buf n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = if atomic-cas buf n 9 then 1 else 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r + z - z

  m-memory-fence : Integer -> Integer
  m-memory-fence (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = memory-fence n
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The CPU Reads And The Rest Of Raw Memory

 THE PORT AND MMIO NAMES ARE NOT HERE AND THE REASON IS THE ARM SHAPE, not
 the cost. `port-in-byte`, the MMIO four and the GPU four all carry an effect
 row -- `Device.Port`, `Device.Mmio`, `Gpu.Compute`, `Gpu.Memory` -- and every
 arm in this chapter is a pure `Integer -> Integer`. Measuring them needs an
 effectful arm and a chapter that declares those effects, which is a change to
 the instrument rather than another row in it. What IS here is everything in
 that neighbourhood that carries `empty-row`.

 `peek-16`, `peek-qword` and `poke-16` are the siblings the Raw Memory section
 above missed: `peek-byte`, `peek-32`, `poke-byte` and `poke-32` were measured
 on 2026-08-19 and these three were not, for no reason but that nobody listed
 them. Same discipline, buffer before the mark.

 THE THREE CPU NAMES ARE CONSTANTS, NOT CALLS. `cpu-read-cr0`, `cpu-read-cr3`
 and `flush-tlb` are typed plain `Integer` with no `FunTy` and no emitter, so
 a use is a bare name. They read `bs-varies` True because the value depends on
 the machine, and on this bed they answer 0x80000013, 0x8000 and 0.

  m-peek-16 : Integer -> Integer
  m-peek-16 (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = peek-16 buf 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-peek-qword : Integer -> Integer
  m-peek-qword (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = peek-qword buf 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-poke-16 : Integer -> Integer
  m-poke-16 (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in let r = poke-16 buf 0 258
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-cpuid : Integer -> Integer
  m-cpuid (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = cpu-cpuid-eax 0 + cpu-cpuid-ebx 0 + cpu-cpuid-ecx 0 + cpu-cpuid-edx 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-cpu-regs : Integer -> Integer
  m-cpu-regs (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = cpu-read-cr0 + cpu-read-cr3 + flush-tlb
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

Section: The Effectful Arms

 EVERY ARM ABOVE THIS LINE IS A PURE `Integer -> Integer` AND THAT IS WHY
 THESE ROWS WAITED. An effectful builtin cannot be bound with `let`
 (CDX2033 says so and names the fix), so an arm that calls one has to open an
 `act` block and take its answer with `<-`. That is a different bracket, and a
 different bracket is a different instrument until something says otherwise.

 `act-control` IS THAT SOMETHING AND IT IS THE FIRST ARM IN THE SECTION. It
 opens the same `act` block, takes the same two marks, and calls nothing
 measured, so whatever the block costs is visible on its own row and every
 reading below is read against it rather than against zero. Without it a
 section where all twelve rows read the same nonzero would be indistinguishable
 from twelve builtins that each allocate that much, and the whole section would
 publish `fixed` on the strength of the bracket.

 WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND IT IS A CHOICE ABOUT THE BED RATHER THAN THE COST.
 `gpu-in`, `gpu-out`, `gpu-mem-read` and `gpu-mem-write` reach the host-side
 rasterizer, and `gpu-mem-write` at offset 0 of the window is where
 `DeviceBuffer` keeps its allocation cursor, so an arm that writes there is
 corrupting an allocator to measure one. The two block forms and
 `runtime-init` are out for the same class of reason. All seven stay
 `unknown`, deliberately, and each is a row somebody can take with a bed arm
 built for it.

 The port arms use **port 0x80**, the POST diagnostic port, which is a sink on
 every machine this runs on. The MMIO arms use our own buffer, which is plain
 RAM, and it is allocated before the mark like every other accessor here.

  m-act-control : Integer -> Integer
  m-act-control (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + buf - buf
    end

  m-read-mmio : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  m-read-mmio (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- read-mmio buf 0
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-read-mmio-32 : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  m-read-mmio-32 (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- read-mmio-32 buf 0
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-poke-mmio : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  m-poke-mmio (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- poke-mmio buf 0 65
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-poke-mmio-32 : Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  m-poke-mmio-32 (n) =
    let buf = alloc-bytes 64
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- poke-mmio-32 buf 0 65
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  post-port : Integer = #0080

  m-port-in-byte : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  m-port-in-byte (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- port-in-byte post-port
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-port-out-byte : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  m-port-out-byte (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- port-out-byte post-port 0
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-port-in-16 : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  m-port-in-16 (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- port-in-16 post-port
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-port-out-16 : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  m-port-out-16 (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- port-out-16 post-port 0
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-port-in-32 : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  m-port-in-32 (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- port-in-32 post-port
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end

  m-port-out-32 : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  m-port-out-32 (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      r <- port-out-32 post-port 0
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + r - r
    end


Section: The Print Family

 THE ASCII PROBE IS NOT THE WHOLE PATH. `emit-print-text-loop` branches on the
 code unit and takes a different arm for anything whose Unicode needs more
 than one byte, so a text of `x` never enters it. A class read off ASCII alone
 would be the fixture-shape trap (L-CONSTRUCT), so two arms below build their
 text from a tier-0 accented code unit and a tier-0 Cyrillic one instead.

  mk-code-text : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  mk-code-text (code) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else mk-code-text code n (i + 1) (acc & char-to-text (code-to-char code))

  m-print-line-accented : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-line-accented (n) =
    let s = mk-code-text 97 n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line-uni s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-line-tier1 : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-line-tier1 (n) =
    let s = mk-code-text 192 n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line-uni s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-line-cyrillic : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-line-cyrillic (n) =
    let s = mk-code-text 113 n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line-uni s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

 Seven builtins, FOUR emitters. `print-line`, `print-line-uni`, `print-error`
 and `print-error-uni` all lower through `emit-print-line-builtin`, so they
 cannot differ in class and the aliases are measured to show that rather than
 assumed. `print-uni` takes `emit-print-no-newline-builtin`, `print-text`
 takes `emit-print-text-builtin`, and `print-line-raw` takes
 `emit-print-line-raw-builtin`.

 Each arm builds its text BEFORE the bracket, so the text is not in the
 reading; what is measured is what one call retains. These return Nothing, so
 the `+ r - r` bracket is not available and is not needed: the call is
 effectful and nothing may eliminate it.

  m-print-line : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-line (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-line-uni : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-line-uni (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line-uni s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-error : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-error (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-error s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-error-uni : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-error-uni (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-error-uni s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-uni : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-uni (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-uni s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-text : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-text (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-text s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end

  m-print-line-raw : Integer -> [Console] Integer
  m-print-line-raw (n) =
    let s = mk-text n 0 ""
    in let b = __heap-save
    in act
      print-line-raw s
      let a = __heap-save in a - b
    end
Section: The Read-Only Process And Identity Rows

 Only the READS. Everything in this neighbourhood that spawns, kills, sets a
 scope, restricts a capability or moves a channel is left alone, because an
 arm that measures a builtin by changing the state of the machine it is
 measured on is not an arm. Those rows want a bed built for them.

 SIX OF THE ELEVEN ARE NULLARY CONSTANTS, like the CPU three above, so the
 registry is never consulted for them and their rows buy no consumer anything
 (see the `machined` control in `codex/test/apps/bounded-none-accepted`). They
 are measured anyway: a row may only carry a value somebody read, and "no
 consumer asks" is not "the answer does not exist".

 TWO ROWS THIS SECTION COULD HAVE TAKEN AND DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.
 `process-get-scope` and `process-get-network-scope` return Text, and on this
 bed both answer the EMPTY string -- `text-length` reads 0 for each. A zero
 length is a fact about a process with no scope set, not about the builtin: a
 non-empty scope is a Text somebody has to build, and building one takes
 `process-set-scope`, which is a mutation this chapter will not make. Reading
 `none` off the empty path would be the corpus-shape failure exactly, so both
 stay `unknown` until an arm can hand them a scope to return.

  m-proc-constants : Integer -> Integer
  m-proc-constants (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = process-yield + get-ticks + process-get-pid + process-count
                 + hardware-random + boot-factstore-status
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-proc-reads : Integer -> Integer
  m-proc-reads (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in let r = process-get-cap 0 + process-status 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in (a - b) + r - r

  m-identity : Integer -> [Identity] Integer
  m-identity (n) =
    let b = __heap-save
    in act
      w <- identity-whoami 0
      d <- device-seed 0
      g <- identity-get-proc 0 0
      let a = __heap-save in (a - b) + (w + d + g) - (w + d + g)
    end

